[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-18", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 18 — the date bound moves into the query and a range on a bound entity becomes a keyed scan; Datalog row 94 → 190 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-18.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 18 - the date bound moves into the query, the engine learns that a range on a bound entity is a keyed scan, and the Datalog row doubles\n\n> Seed: iteration 17's S7 - write IC02/IC09's date bound into the query\n> so fuse-value-ranges prunes before the join, and the top-20 into\n> :order-by/:limit. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2, rung SF1 subset,\n> origin :cross-host (simeon), mix IC02/07/08/09, impl :datalog, 8 slots\n> (reference 93.9 ops/s, iteration 17).\n\n## Generate\n\n  Prediction: IC09 empty answers ~5 ms (the range is empty on this\n  subset for every official maxDate); IC02 near the index twin; the\n  Datalog row toward the rtt ceiling (~250 at 8 slots).\n\n  Read before writing: datalog.core's range branch groups bindings by\n  (a lo hi e) and runs the relation-wide scan-range* once per group, then\n  filters by e. With ?msg already BOUND from the join, that is one walk of\n  every creationDate value (86,932) per distinct message. Written as\n  seeded, the fused range would have made IC09 SLOWER than the harness\n  filter it replaced. So the engine had to move first.\n\n## Evolve\n\n  - kotoba-lang/datalog da95b877: a range on a bound entity is a keyed\n    scan [e a _] + in-range?; an open entity keeps the relation cut; the\n    async branch the same. Test with a counting IPatternSource/\n    IRangeSource stub: bound entity => 0 range walks, one keyed scan per\n    entity; open entity => exactly one range cut; same answer. Shown\n    failing on the old engine (20 walks for 20 entities). 124/237/0.\n  - kotobase-peer deps.edn: datalog pinned at that sha as an explicit\n    floor (a base library's fix does not reach the deployer until someone\n    names it). datalog main is .cljk now; the loader mirrors gitlibs\n    entries too, so it loads. kotobase-peer suite on the pin: 261/798/0.\n  - kb-ic02 / kb-ic09: `[(< ?date max)]` (the string form -- creationDate\n    is stored as a 13-digit string, string order is date order) plus\n    :order-by [[?date :desc] [?msg :asc]] :limit 20 inside the query.\n    IC09 keeps its two queries, each limited to 20: the union's top 20 is\n    a subset of the union of the two top-20s, so limiting each is exact.\n    The planner refuses to reorder a query with a predicate, so the\n    written clause order is the plan.\n\n## Rank\n\n    in-process, official parameters, 12 warm passes, p50 of 7:\n                 before          after\n    ic02 empty   2.5   ->   0.8      nonempty 8.1 -> 1.1\n    ic09 empty   163.7 ->   63.1\n    ic07/ic08    unchanged (1.7 / 2.0)\n\n    served, cross-host, 8 slots, impl datalog (gate: 392/392 agree with\n    the index twins, which iteration 16 validated against the old Datalog):\n    rate  on-time%  thru/s  ic02  ic07  ic08   ic09\n     96   100.00    95.6   11.1  11.9  12.3   85.9\n    128   100.00   127.4   11.0  11.8  12.0   91.3\n    192   100.00   190.2   12.5  13.7  14.3  109.2   <- highest passing\n    256    16.31   197.9\n\n    throughput 93.9 -> 190.2 ops/s   target 139,411 -> ratio 733 (raw)\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction half held. The row doubled and IC02 reached the index\n    twin, but IC09 stopped at 63 ms in-process / 86-109 served, not 5.\n    The range prunes ROWS after `[?msg hasCreator ?x]` has produced them;\n    the two-hop join still materialises friend-of-friend x messages. A\n    range clause cannot shrink the join that feeds it unless it runs\n    first, and running it first is right only when the range is small --\n    which the engine cannot know without an ordered value index (S6).\n  - The seam finding repeats iteration 17's shape: a feature that was\n    correct and slow in the one case it was built for (a bound entity is\n    the common case for a fused range in a join). Both were found by\n    reading the branch before running it, and both were then measured.\n  - The remaining gap to the index column (255.6 at 8 slots) is IC09\n    alone: 109 ms Datalog vs 41 index. Same answer, 2.7x the work.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH on the Datalog column\n(93.9 -> 190.2, rung/origin/mix/inflight/impl held). Index column\nunchanged.\n\nSeeds for iteration 19, in order:\n\n  - S6 (ordered value index): a sorted view of creationDate on the hot db\n    so the range is a cut and a top-k walk can stop at 20 -- then IC09 can\n    run range-first when the bound is early, and the top-20 does not\n    materialise the rest. Predicted: IC09 Datalog -> ~15 ms, row -> the\n    rtt ceiling (~250 at 8 slots), and the index column too.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 on simeon (16 GiB; the 35k load takes 8 s there).\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7.\n  - S8 (wire): IC02/07/08 sit at the rtt; 32 slots showed 511 on the\n    index column -- the driver-thread count is the sponsor's choice and\n    the rows should say which column they are.\n"}]
